if (data & 0x80) {
  note = data & 0x7F;
  if (data & 0x40) instrument = next byte;
  if (data & 0x20) volume = next byte;
  if (data & 0x10) effect = next byte;
  if (effect) param = next byte;
} else {
  note = data; instrument = next; volume = next; effect = next; param = next;
}
```
Wait bit 7 is "packed" and bits 0-6 note? Let's check. In XM, pattern cell packing:
- bit 7: packed
- bit 6: instrument
- bit 5: volume
- bit 4: effect
- bits 0-3: note
If packed, note is in low 5 bits? Actually note can be 0-97, so needs 7 bits. The note is always read as a full byte if packed? Let's recall from libxm: 
```
if (b & 0x80) {
  note = b & 0x7F;
  if (b & 0x40) ins = *p++;
  if (b & 0x20) vol = *p++;
  if (b & 0x10) eff = *p++;
  if (eff) par = *p++;
} else {
  note = b; ins = *p++; vol = *p++; eff = *p++; par = *p++;
}
```
Yes. Let's parse. We can also inspect `pattern_get_cell` for a few known cells to validate. Let's write parser and output a summary of notes per pattern, and check for cells with note-off etc. We'll also check for duplicate row/channel writes? The XM only stores one cell per row/channel, so no duplicates. We need check for unintended overwrites: our composition order may have overwritten arp with harmony etc. We fixed by channel separation. But there may still be conflicts between `bell` and `fx` on ch11, or `crash` on ch1 and snare at row 0 (no), or `lead` and `harmony` both on ch6/ch7 (different). Let's parse and inspect per pattern channel usage.

